---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import StatsBar from '../components/StatsBar.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<BaseLayout>
  <main class="pt-[72px]">
    <Hero />
    <section id="about" class="pt-lg pb-xl md:py-xl max-w-content mx-auto px-margin-mobile md:px-margin-desktop scroll-mt-[80px]">
      <StatsBar />
      <div class="mt-xl">
        <span class="font-label text-label-sm text-primary mb-sm inline-block uppercase tracking-widest">About me</span>
        <h2 class="font-display text-display-lg-mobile md:text-display-lg font-extrabold mb-lg">Who I Am</h2>
        <div class="max-w-2xl flex flex-col gap-md">
          <p class="font-body text-body-lg text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            I'm a Graduate economics student at NUS, interested in the interaction between market
            dynamics and human behaviour. I'm really into people-centric roles where I can observe
            and ideate tangentially.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-body-lg text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            I love to understand what goes on in people's brains when they make decisions and why
            they so often stray from what economic theory predicts. Lately I've been thinking about
            how cognitive inconsistencies shape microeconomic decisions and how they aggregate on
            the larger scale.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-body-lg text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            I came into economics from a non-math background, which meant I had to build the
            quantitative side from scratch. Somewhere between real analysis and stochastic
            processes, I stopped finding that intimidating and started finding it genuinely fun. My
            coursework at Madras School of Economics covered a wide range like differential
            equations, time series, game theory, applied econometrics, and I ended up TA-ing
            Mathematical Methods for Economics II and Python Programming, which I think says more
            about stubbornness than talent.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-body-lg text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            I'm looking to work in fintech analytics, investment research, or behaviour-based
            policy. If the role involves turning messy, complex data into inferring and making
            sharper decisions, count me in.
          </p>
          <p class="font-body text-body-lg text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
            Please click on <a href={`${base}/portfolio`} class="text-primary underline">portfolio</a> to
            find my projects and feel free to explore my social links in my contact.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
